  "comments": {
    "c1": {
      "anchor": "short#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "1. The short version",
      "excerpt": "Solid 2's headline features are unused here. Stores, projections, optimistic val",
      "text": "I was referring to how components and spell apps are written, not the editor.  I think this may still hold if we take \"framework\" to mean \"the equivalent of e.g. svelte/view/react.\" \n\nYes `lit` exists, but it isn't a framework -- it's a way of creating elements, but nothing around that.\n\nThe examples (Photoshop etc) are not frameworks, they are \"apps.\"  And they don't let you build other things, they are standalone apps.",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T22:41:06.405Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "The first framework where the entire app is web components",
      "offset": 2950,
      "edited": "2026-11-20T22:42:31.334Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T23:52:17.784Z",
          "html": "<p>Agreed: with \"framework\" meaning React / Vue / Svelte's kind, Photoshop, YouTube and the rest drop out (they're apps), and so does Lit (it only makes elements). The claim gets much closer, but \"first\" still doesn't hold.</p>\n<p>Frameworks whose apps are written as custom elements, before Spell:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Polymer + App Toolbox</b> (2016): routing, layout, storage, build, app templates; the app is one element. Now in maintenance mode.</li>\n  <li><b>Salesforce LWC + LWR</b>: components are \"custom elements\"; LWR adds routes, a router, server data, server rendering. Tied to Salesforce.</li>\n  <li><b>Enhance</b>: \"HTML-first full-stack web framework\": pages, API routes, a store, server-rendered elements.</li>\n  <li><b>Hybrids</b>: \"a framework for the web\": store, routing, layout, translated text.</li>\n  <li><b>Imba</b>: a compiled language whose components become native web components (you name the tags).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Not counted: Stencil (\"primarily ... component libraries\"), Vaadin (you write Java or React), Lit, FAST, Atomico.</p>\n<p>What none of them does: you never write an element (your types become them), the tags are in the program's own language, and one element layer runs under the kit, the docs, the editor and every program.</p>\n<p><b>Suggested line:</b> \"Spell is a web-component-first framework: every view of every app you write is a real HTML element, named after your own types.\" Say \"every view\", not \"all the way down\": inside each element is Solid.</p>\n<p>The page's short version and 8.1 now say this, with sources.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "done": {
        "how": "good",
        "at": "2026-11-20T23:55:29.565Z",
        "by": "owenoak"
      },
